    "closed": true,
    "closedAt": "2026-04-06T17:00:26Z",
    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "The usual suggested approach of [automated disabling](https://nvim-mini.org/mini.nvim/doc/mini-nvim.html#mini.nvim-disabling-recipes-automateddisabling) should work here: find a way to automatically run a code for a buffer in which you want to disable something and set `vim.b.miniindentscope_disable = true`.\r\n\r\nIn this case, it doesn't look like the diagnostic's buffer has any distinctive features (filetype and buffer name are empty). But `vim.diagnostic.open_float` returns a buffer identifier displayed in the opened window. So creating a wrapper function for `vim.diagnostic.open_float()` that captures that buffer id and then sets `vim.b.miniindentscope_disable` should do the trick. Something like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n_G.diag_open_float = function(...)\r\n  local buf_id = vim.diagnostic.open_float(...)\r\n  vim.b[buf_id].miniindentscope_disable = true\r\nend\r\nvim.keymap.set('n', '<Leader>ld', diag_open_float)\r\n```",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">The usual suggested approach of <a href=\"https://nvim-mini.org/mini.nvim/doc/mini-nvim.html#mini.nvim-disabling-recipes-automateddisabling\" rel=\"nofollow\">automated disabling</a> should work here: find a way to automatically run a code for a buffer in which you want to disable something and set <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.b.miniindentscope_disable = true</code>.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">In this case, it doesn't look like the diagnostic's buffer has any distinctive features (filetype and buffer name are empty). But <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.diagnostic.open_float</code> returns a buffer identifier displayed in the opened window. So creating a wrapper function for <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.diagnostic.open_float()</code> that captures that buffer id and then sets <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.b.miniindentscope_disable</code> should do the trick. Something like this:</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"_G.diag_open_float = function(...)\n  local buf_id = vim.diagnostic.open_float(...)\n  vim.b[buf_id].miniindentscope_disable = true\nend\nvim.keymap.set('n', '&lt;Leader&gt;ld', diag_open_float)\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-c1\">_G</span>.<span class=\"pl-en\">diag_open_float</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>(...)\n  <span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">buf_id</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">diagnostic</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">open_float</span>(<span class=\"pl-c1\">...</span>)\n  <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">b</span>[<span class=\"pl-smi\">buf_id</span>].<span class=\"pl-e\">miniindentscope_disable</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">true</span>\n<span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n<span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">keymap</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">set</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>n<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>&lt;Leader&gt;ld<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">diag_open_float</span>)</pre></div>",
            "bodyText": "The usual suggested approach of automated disabling should work here: find a way to automatically run a code for a buffer in which you want to disable something and set vim.b.miniindentscope_disable = true.\nIn this case, it doesn't look like the diagnostic's buffer has any distinctive features (filetype and buffer name are empty). But vim.diagnostic.open_float returns a buffer identifier displayed in the opened window. So creating a wrapper function for vim.diagnostic.open_float() that captures that buffer id and then sets vim.b.miniindentscope_disable should do the trick. Something like this:\n_G.diag_open_float = function(...)\n  local buf_id = vim.diagnostic.open_float(...)\n  vim.b[buf_id].miniindentscope_disable = true\nend\nvim.keymap.set('n', '<Leader>ld', diag_open_float)",
